  "dom_id": "step-q47686",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Which of the following functions is not differentiable over the interval $(-1,1)$ due to the existence of a vertical asymptote at a point in this interval?",
  "worked_solution": "The function $y=g(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and is  therefore not differentiable at $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ So $y=g(x)$ is the correct answer.\n\nThe other graphs do not have a a vertical asymptote inside the interval $(-1,1).$\n\n- The function $y=p(x)$ has a corner point at $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and is therefore not differentiable at $x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The function $y=h(x)$ has a vertical tangent at $x=0$ and is therefore not differentiable at $x=0.$\n\n- The function $y=q(x)$ is smooth and continuous over the entire interval $x\\in (-1,1)$ and is therefore differentiable at every point in the interval.\n\n- The function $y=f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and is therefore not differentiable at $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$",
